The app ids were read off each game's own Steam store page on 2026-09-22
(`store.steampowered.com/app/<id>`). Modern Warfare III's old id 2519060 now
redirects to 3595270, which is the one above.

Newer Steam apps keep their capsules under a content hash, so the plain
`apps/<id>/header.jpg` answers 404 for them (MW3 and NBA 2K26 did). The
exact URL of the header is the `header_image` field of Steam's own store
API, `store.steampowered.com/api/appdetails?appids=<id>`. That field has no
library capsule, so the tall rows come from Steam's other store API,
`api.steampowered.com/IStoreBrowseService/GetItems/v1` with
`"data_request": {"include_assets": true}`, whose `assets` block names
`library_capsule_2x` (and every other capsule) with its hash folder; the
file is `store_item_assets/` plus `asset_url_format` with `${FILENAME}`
filled in, without the `?t=` stamp. Read on 2026-09-23. The hash changes
when the publisher replaces the art, so a row that starts answering 404
later needs the same lookup again.

**Warzone's tall picture is its vertical capsule**, not its library capsule.
Steam's store API names no library capsule for app 1962663, and the plain
`library_600x900_2x.jpg` still served there is the retired 2022 art
("MW | WARZONE", the Modern Warfare II era). The vertical capsule
(`hero_capsule_alt_assets_20_2x.jpg`, 748 x 896, about 0.83:1) is the
current Warzone art with "CALL OF DUTY WARZONE" on it, so it is the one. It
is less tall than 2:3, and the tall slot draws it whole with soft edges
above and below.

**Black Ops 7's tall picture is its vertical capsule too, for the same
reason.** Its app id, 3606480, was read off its store page
(`store.steampowered.com/app/3606480`) on 2026-09-23. The header row is the
`header_image` of Steam's `appdetails` API, less its `?t=` stamp. Steam's
`GetItems` API names no library capsule for this app (with or without
overrides), and the plain `library_600x900_2x.jpg` answers 404 on every
Steam CDN host, so the tall row is the `hero_capsule_2x` that API does name:
Steam's vertical capsule, 374 x 448 at 2x, the same shape as Warzone's. Both
rows say `not yet` until `fetch-pack-art.ps1` has fetched them; the script
refuses a picture whose real size is not the row's shape, so the 748 x 896
here is checked on the way in. Until then the app falls back to our own
banner for this pack (D121: a picture is only used once it has loaded).

## The two from the PlayStation Store

Fortnite and MLB The Show 25 are not sold on Steam, and Epic's own store
refuses a script (403), so both come from the PlayStation Store, where each
game's publisher uploads its art. The URLs are images of each game's concept
page (`store.playstation.com/en-us/concept/228748` for Fortnite,
`.../concept/10011213` for MLB The Show 25): the `SIXTEEN_BY_NINE_BANNER`
for `wide`, asked for at `?w=460`, which the image service answers at
504 x 284, and the `PORTRAIT_BANNER` (2:3) for `tall`, asked for at `?w=600`,
which it answers at 764 x 1146 and the script scales to 600 x 900. Each tall
picture is from the same set as its wide one (the same `rnd/<date>/<time>`
folder), so the two agree. Fortnite's art is its CURRENT season's and Epic
replaces it every season; the files here are the ones fetched on the dates
in the table, and a new season's pictures are the same lookup again.

## Missing

Five packs added on 2026-10-10 have no picture yet (`art: null`), so the app
draws our own banner for them. No artwork was fetched for them: the owner's
rule for that round was no official game art taken from the web. To add one,
put its rows in the table above (Steam's header and 2:3 capsule where Steam
sells the game; VALORANT is not on Steam) and run `fetch-pack-art.ps1` once
the owner has said yes.

- Overwatch
- VALORANT
- Marvel Rivals
- Battlefield 6
- ARC Raiders
- Call of Duty: Black Ops 6
- PUBG: Battlegrounds
- The Finals
- Marathon
- Rust

The last five were added on 2026-10-15 by `import-xim-translators.js` (D394),
for games whose turn speeds XIM's data carries; no art was fetched for them.

Every other pack names its game's own pictures in the table above, and all
of them are fetched (Black Ops 7's two on 2026-09-23). A row marked
`not yet` would be waiting for the script: until its file is in this folder
a slot of that shape draws the pack's other picture, whole, and with
neither there, our own banner.
